// Regression fixture: a LIVE "failed to transpile" stub must FAIL THE BUILD,
// not produce a binary that aborts with no diagnostic.
//
// The derive rule below splices a body that calls a method no type has.
// `.to_expr()` re-parses the body, the method miss is thrown inside the
// spliced closure body, and the closure-body trial SWALLOWS it — codegen then
// emits an `abort()` stub for the function. (It used to name an UNBOUND
// identifier instead; since 2026-09-12 an unbound name is a hard swallow
// class and fails `check` outright —
// issues/fixed/async-closure-body-unknown-identifier-passes-check.md — so a
// method miss is what still reaches the stub path.)
//
// Codegen guards that with GNU's `error` attribute, making the C toolchain the
// deadness oracle: a dead stub links clean, a live one must fail. But that
// attribute is diagnosed from the BACKEND, after optimization, so at -O2 —
// which is every real build — LLVM folded the call to the noreturn stub away
// and the diagnostic never fired. The program compiled clean and died at
// rc=134 with no output.
// issues/fixed/ftt-stub-error-attribute-does-not-fire-at-O2.md
//
// The stub body now also calls an UNDEFINED extern symbol, so a surviving call
// leaves an unresolved reference and the LINK fails at every optimization
// level. This build must therefore fail, naming `failed_to_transpile`.
{ println } :: import("std/fmt");
{ String } :: import("std/string");
Lbl :: trait(lbl : (fn(inout(self) : Self) -> String));
__r :: (
  fn(comptime(T) : Type, comptime(ctx) : DeriveContext, comptime(tp) : ComptimeList(Expr)) -> comptime(Expr)
)({
  src :: "String.from(\"x\").__no_such_method_at_the_splice_site()";
  ctx.make_impl(quote(Lbl(lbl : ((self) -> #(src.to_expr())))))
});
derive_rule(Lbl, __r);
P :: struct(x : i32);
derive(P, Lbl);
main :: (fn() -> unit)(println(P(x : i32(1)).lbl()));
export(main);
